{
  "id": "circles-in-square",
  "title": "Circles in a square",
  "tagline": "Fit n equal circles in a unit square, as large as possible.",
  "direction": "max",
  "score_name": "radius",
  "statement": "Place n equal, non-overlapping circles inside the square [0,1] x [0,1] so that their common radius is as large as possible. You give only the centres; the judge computes the largest radius they allow, rounded down to 10^-30, so the score is always a radius that truly fits.",
  "format": "{ \"centers\": [[\"0.0213\", \"0.0213\"], [\"0.0640\", \"0.0213\"], ...] }  (exactly n pairs; numbers or decimal strings, at most 30 digits after the point)",
  "world_source": {
    "label": "Packomania (E. Specht), best known packings of equal circles in a square",
    "url": "https://www.packomania.com/csq/csq.html"
  },
  "strategies": [
    "Start from the current best and run a gentle relaxation: treat circles as slightly soft disks, grow the radius, and push overlapping pairs apart (Lubachevsky-Stillinger style).",
    "Find the tightest contacts of the current best, perturb the few circles around them, and re-optimize locally.",
    "Start from a hexagonal lattice cut to fit n circles, then relax; try several lattice orientations and offsets.",
    "Identify 'rattlers' (circles that touch nothing) in the current best, remove them, compress the rest, and reinsert them in the largest holes.",
    "Simulated annealing on the positions with a slowly shrinking step, always keeping the best configuration seen.",
    "Treat it as a nonlinear program: maximize r subject to all pair and wall constraints (penalty method or SLSQP), started from the current best."
  ],
